description:
Removes the temporary assumption made in fz-sc8.9, where every extern is treated as pure and any extern may be called from a guard.

Fact: many externs have effects:
- fz_dbg_value prints;
- fz_send and fz_spawn are process operations;
- fz_self reads the current process;
- fz_panic aborts;
- fz_make_ref, fz_make_resource and fz_resource_claim create or claim resources;
- fz_process_heap_alloc_stats reads ambient heap state;
- fz_binary_to_atom interns into the atom table;
- every libc::* call in lib/file.fz does file I/O.
The epic adds more: halt, IO.puts, and the line reads behind File.stream!.

Shape (from the fz-sc8.9 review): the extern declaration carries the mark. The candidate is `extern "C" defguardp ...` (Elixir's word for usable in guards); an `@pure` attribute would need a parser head (frontdoor.rs:187), a decode arm and function-attr routing (quoted_surface.rs:211). The extern quoted map's unread `private` key becomes the read mark. Guard resolution accepts only marked externs, and the refusal names the extern: "not guard-safe".

Acceptance: restores the fixture fz-sc8.9 retired, `00488_receive_impure_guard` (purpose: a receive guard calling an impure helper is a compile-time error; expect: diagnostic). Its program, verbatim:

    def helper(), do: make_ref()
    def rx() do
      receive do
        {:foo, _} when helper() -> 0
      end
    end
    def main() do rx() end

It returns with the diagnostic code this ticket's refusal produces (it was `lower/unsupported` before guards could call externs), and fails on all three paths until `fz_make_ref` is refused as not guard-safe.
